                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\"UFHO2\" is a mobile app that was dumped on Steam 12 years ago, as a paid product.  It was later removed from the mobile app stores, most likely due to the low quality of the product.  Apple and Google have QA departments, Valve has \"Steam Direct\". \n\nWhat do you do in the game?  This mobile app is basically a ripoff of board games like \"Downfall\", and like \"Downfall\", gameplay is an activity of rotating cogs around to create pathways for little meeples to move around.  It wasn't successful on the app stores or on Steam. \n\nThis is clearly a mobile app... it makes no difference if this hit PC before the app stores, or if it never even launched on a phone.  This is built for the absolutely lowest of all gaming platforms and must be judged as such... it's not going to give you a cutting edge PC gaming experience.  That fact by itself is plenty to disqualify this from being recommended to gamers on Steam.  We don't spend thousands building gaming rigs so that we can pretend they're iPhones and play iPhone games and mobile apps.\n\nBecause this was designed for mobile phones, not gaming PCs, the 3D graphics are relatively low poly and don't look great, it's not up to standard for what we expect from a professionally developed PC game.  When a game is designed to run on 5 year old iPhones, it's obviously not going to come close to the minimum quality standards that PC gaming enthusiasts expect on their cutting edge gaming rigs.  Mobile devs have made gaming worse for everyone by dumping mobile garbage on PC.  We don't spend thousands on a gaming rig so we can pretend it's an iPhone.\n\nThis game is ancient, it's over 11 years old... it's heavily dated and isn't going to give you a remotely contemporary gaming experience... another reason for gamers to be hesitant about adding this to their libraries.\n\nLet's talk about pricing and the actual value this has for gamers.\n\nThere's no initial download price, but the developer was charging $10 USD for this.\n\nPrice barriers, especially on low quality products, can be a direct cause of failure.  It seems like a nice gesture for the game to have no up-front cost anymore, but it also tells us that the game wasn't good enough to succeed as a paid product.  This is also a bit of a slap in the face to anyone who showed enough questionable judgement to pay money for this... no discernable effort to refund anyone was made.\n\nIn terms of player counts and popular reception, this couldn't even crack 100 players at its absolute peak. For a free game on a platform with 130 million players, that is a spectacular failure, mostly due to the reasons I've supplied here.\n\nBearing in mind that free games don't add +1 to your game collector count, should you bother adding this to your library and downloading it?\n\nNot this time.  This isn't worth downloading.  While this won't cost you any money, you get what you pay for, and sometimes, even when the game is free, it's still not enough.",
